From: bigjakk <6015188+bigjakk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:00:00 -0500
Subject: kcc: cheaper WM_INPUT handling while raw mouse input is in use

Two independent savings in ui/views/win/hwnd_message_handler.cc for the
time pointer lock with unadjustedMovement is held (using_wm_input_ true).
Both run on the browser UI thread once per mouse report (1000-8000 Hz).

1. HWNDMessageHandler::OnInputEvent

Stock: every WM_INPUT costs two GetRawInputData() calls (a size query with
a null buffer, then the read) and one heap allocation:

  auto buffer = std::make_unique<uint8_t[]>(size);

With the feature enabled the packet is read with a single GetRawInputData()
call into a RAWINPUT on the stack. RAWINPUT's data union is sized by
RAWMOUSE, so a mouse packet is exactly sizeof(RAWINPUT) bytes. The stack
copy is used only if the call returns exactly sizeof(RAWINPUT); if it
returns (UINT)-1 (for example ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_BUFFER for a larger HID
packet) or any other size, the code falls through to the unchanged stock
two-call path, which also keeps the stock error logging. Calling
GetRawInputData() again for the same handle is what stock already does.
The rest of the handler (button state, event construction,
DefRawInputProc) is shared and unchanged. The stock lines are only
re-indented into an "if (!input)" block.

2. HWNDMessageHandler::HandleMouseEventInternal

Raw input is registered without RIDEV_NOLEGACY, so Windows still posts a
legacy WM_MOUSEMOVE for the (hidden, clipped) cursor. Stock drops it late:

  if (using_wm_input_ && (event.type() == ui::EventType::kMouseMoved ||
                          event.type() == ui::EventType::kMouseDragged)) {
    return 0;
  }

Before reaching that line it has already built a ui::MouseEvent from the
message (event type/flags/location from the MSG, several GetKeyState()
calls for modifiers and lock keys, two clock reads, GetTickCount(), latency
info) and run IsSynthesizedMouseMessage(). With the feature enabled a
WM_MOUSEMOVE received while using_wm_input_ returns 0 just before that
construction. What stock does for such a message before dropping it, and
how the early return treats it:
  - pen-active filter and the touch-synthesized-message block at the top of
    the function: still run, the early return sits after them;
  - TrackMouseEvents(TME_LEAVE) when the message is a plain move (no button
    in wParam, i.e. EventType::kMouseMoved), track_mouse is set and the
    window does not hold capture: kept, same condition;
  - return value 0 with the message left marked handled: same;
  - building the ui::MouseEvent: skipped. Its only lasting effect for a
    move is refreshing the function-static "last_known_location" inside
    ui::EventLocationFromMSG(), a fallback position used for a later
    WM_MOUSELEAVE or scroll message if GetCursorPos() fails. Other mouse
    messages keep refreshing it. Not preserved on purpose.
No tooltip, activity or delegate call happens on the stock path before the
drop, so nothing else is lost. WM_NCMOUSEMOVE is left on the stock path
(the cursor is clipped to the client area while locked, so it is not hot).
Messages forwarded from the legacy child HWND arrive through
HandleMouseMessage() with track_mouse == false and take the same early
return.

GetRawInputBuffer() batching was considered and left out: it drains the
pending raw packets ahead of the legacy button, key and wheel messages
queued between them, and the page receives presses from those legacy
messages, so motion made after a click could be delivered before the click.
I could not show that this is safe.

Gating: base::Feature KccRawInputFastPath, disabled by default, defined in
the anonymous namespace of hwnd_message_handler.cc and read once. Browser
process only. With the feature off OnInputEvent takes the stock two-call
path and HandleMouseEventInternal is unchanged.

A/B: --enable-features=KccRawInputFastPath. With an 8000 Hz mouse moving
continuously under pointer lock, compare browser-process UI-thread CPU
(ETW/WPA: time under HWNDMessageHandler::OnInputEvent and
HandleMouseEventInternal, NtUserGetRawInputData call count). Expect a small
gain: one syscall and one malloc/free per report, plus the event
construction per legacy move; none of this was measured. Functional checks:
movementX/Y sums unchanged, buttons and wheel unchanged, mouseleave still
fires after pointer lock ends and the cursor leaves the window.

Platforms: Windows only.

Not verified: nothing was compiled. Whether Windows keeps posting
WM_MOUSEMOVE at the full report rate while the hidden cursor is pinned
against the clip rectangle was not measured; if it does not, part 2 simply
runs less often.

diff --git a/ui/views/win/hwnd_message_handler.cc b/ui/views/win/hwnd_message_handler.cc
index b0eed3081bea1f23c23894b16825a1671af01ddb..bedcf05936234846ff8c9b5cc1c917a03331bf7f 100644
--- a/ui/views/win/hwnd_message_handler.cc
+++ b/ui/views/win/hwnd_message_handler.cc
@@ -323,6 +323,18 @@ bool RawWheelPacketHasMotion(const RAWMOUSE& mouse) {
          (mouse.lLastX != 0 || mouse.lLastY != 0);
 }
 
+// kcc: Cheaper per-packet handling while raw mouse input (WM_INPUT) is in use:
+// one GetRawInputData() call into a stack buffer instead of a size query plus
+// a heap allocation, and no ui::MouseEvent construction for the legacy
+// WM_MOUSEMOVE messages that are dropped anyway.
+BASE_FEATURE(kKccRawInputFastPath, base::FEATURE_DISABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
+
+bool RawInputFastPathEnabled() {
+  static const bool enabled =
+      base::FeatureList::IsEnabled(kKccRawInputFastPath);
+  return enabled;
+}
+
 }  // namespace
 
